===Serbia vs Ghana===
Ghana headed into their opening fixture against Serbia as the youngest team in the tournament.<ref name="NYTimesSerbiaGhana">{{Cite web |website=The New York Times |last=Witz |first=Billy |date=13 June 2010 |title=Ghana Gets Helping Hand From Serbia |url=https://archive.nytimes.com/goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/06/13/live-analysis-serbia-vs-ghana}}</ref> Serbia were touted as a potential [[dark horse]] for the tournament,<ref name="NYTimesSerbiaGhana"/> and were looking to improve on their previous [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006]] appearance at the World Cup as Serbia & Montenegro, where they lost all three of their group games and had the worst goal difference of [[2006 FIFA World Cup Group C|minus 8]] in that tournament.<ref name="NYTimesSerbiaGhana"/>

In the opening half, [[Kwadwo Asamoah]] came closest to opening the scoring for Ghana on 15 minutes but [[Nikola Žigić]] to deflected the shot to go wide.<ref name="UEFASerbiaGhana">{{Cite web |website=UEFA |date=13 June 2010 |title=Gyan on the spot for victorious Ghana |url=https://www.uefa.com/european-qualifiers/news/01e6-0e74b2fd01f7-2263599f1674-1000--gyan-on-the-spot-for-victorious-ghana/}}</ref> Serbia created little from open play, but their main threat seemed to be from set pieces.<ref name="UEFASerbiaGhana"/> After half-time, [[Asamoah Gyan|Asamoah Gyan's]] headed the ball towards the Serbia goal from close-range, but it struck the post.<ref name="UEFASerbiaGhana"/>

Serbia were reduced to 10 men when [[Aleksandar Luković]] was sent off for holding back Gyan on the half-way line, which was his second bookable offence.<ref name="BBCGhanaSerbia">{{Cite web |website=BBC Sport |last=Soneji |first=Pranav |date=2002 |title=Serbia 0-1 Ghana |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_07/default.stm}}</ref>

Late in the second half, Serbia substitute [[Zdravko Kuzmanović]] handballed in the box whilst attempting to clear a cross. Gyan converted the resultant penalty kick, giving Ghana a 1–0 lead. Deep into stoppage time, Gyan almost had a second goal, but his strike hit the post of the Serbia goal.<ref name="BBCGhanaSerbia"/>

The victory for Ghana was the first in four games for African countries at the tournament, since South Africa drew with Mexico and Algeria and Nigeria both lost their opening games.<ref name="NYTimesSerbiaGhana"/>
