===Germany vs Australia===
Veteran Germany skipper [[Michael Ballack]] was ruled out of the tournament with injury, and coach Joachim Loew opted for the second-youngest squad in the competition.<ref >{{Cite web |website=BBC News|last=Lyon |first=Sam |date=2010 |title=Germany 4-0 Australia |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_08/default.stm}}</ref>

Prior to the game, Australia coach Pim Verbeek made the surprising decision to bench veteran players [[Harry Kewell]], [[Mark Bresciano]] and [[Joshua Kennedy]], instead opting to put [[Tim Cahill]] in a lone striker role with [[Richard Garcia]] in support.<ref name="ABC1">{{Cite web |website=ABC News |date=14 Jun 2010 |title=Australia on the ropes against Germany |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2010-06-14/australia-on-the-ropes-against-germany/865776}}</ref>

Australia had the chance to score an early goal from a corner in the third minute, but Tim Cahill's header was blocked by Lucas Neill, and Richard Garcia failed to score from the follow up.<ref name="ABC1"/>

[[Lukas Podolski]] opened the scoring for Germany in the eighth minute and [[Miroslav Klose]] put Germany 2–0 up in the 26th minute. After half-time, Tim Cahill was given a straight red card for a challenge on [[Bastian Schweinsteiger]], and [[Thomas Müller]] and substitute [[Cacau]] both scored, completing the 4–0 victory for Germany,<ref >{{Cite web |website=ABC News |last=Paine |first=Chris |date=14 Jun 2010 |title=Socceroos slump to Durban disaster |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2010-06-14/socceroos-slump-to-durban-disaster/865848}}</ref> which marked its sixth successive opening match victory at the World Cup.<ref >{{Cite web |website=The Guardian| last=Taylor |first=Daniel |date=14 Jun 2010 |title=World Cup 2010: Miroslav Klose helps Germany put four past Australia |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/13/germany-australia-world-cup-match-report|quote=This is the sixth successive tournament in which they have won their opening game and they did so in a manner that suggests they must be immune to the nerves that have afflicted other teams in the opening days of the event.}}</ref>

After the match, despite the heavy defeat, Australia coach Pim Verbeek stood by his tactics to keep his veteran players benched.<ref >{{Cite web |website=ABC News |date=14 Jun 2010 |title=Pim takes rap for German blitz |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2010-06-14/pim-takes-rap-for-german-blitz/866340}}</ref> Germany coach Joachim Loew described the victory as "a good warm-up" for his team.<ref >{{Cite web |website=ABC News |date=14 Jun 2010 |title=Socceroos reel after German masterclass |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2010-06-14/socceroos-reel-after-german-masterclass/866044}}</ref>

In 2011, [[Special Broadcasting Service|SBS]] (Australia's World Cup broadcaster) soccer analyst [[Les Murray (broadcaster)|Les Murray]] published a book titled ''The World Game: The Story of How Football Went Global'', in which Murray cited an undisclosed source in alleging that [[Lucas Neill]], the captain of Australia, had instigated a mutiny just before kick-off.<ref>{{cite web |title=Les Murray: When SBS commentator claimed Lucas Neill led a Socceroos mutiny during World Cup |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2017-07-31/when-les-murray-claimed-lucas-neill-led-a-socceroos-mutiny/8760358 |website=ABC News |publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation |access-date=4 January 2026}}</ref> Murray alleged that Neill had asked the coach, Pim Verbeek, to leave the room, before describing Verbeek's game-plan as "bullshit" and erasing what the Dutchman had written on a whiteboard, telling the team to play like they normally do.<ref>{{cite web |title=Murray claims Socceroos World Cup revolt |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2011-06-22/murray-claims-socceroos-world-cup-revolt/2768120 |website=ABC News |publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation |access-date=4 January 2026}}</ref> Neill protested that before the Germany game it was [[Mark Schwarzer]], and not Neill himself, who had given the team pep talk.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Gatt |first1=Ray |title=Furious Lucas Neill denies Les Murray's mutiny claims |url=https://www.theaustralian.com.au/sport/furious-lucas-neill-denies-les-murrays-mutiny-claims/news-story/9a636b211207c574421e1d7375ec358d |website=The Australian |publisher=News Corp |access-date=4 January 2026}}</ref> A few days after the allegations hit the news, Murray retracted his allegations with a full apology, with an undertaking that future editions of his book would have the relevant portion deleted.<ref>{{cite web |title=Neill accepts apology over mutiny allegations |url=https://www.foxsports.com/stories/soccer/neill-accepts-apology-over-mutiny-allegations |website=Fox Sports |access-date=4 January 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Weiner |first1=David |title=Les Murray retracts allegations about Lucas Neill's leadership, Socceroos captain accepts his apology |url=https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/socceroos/veteran-broadcaster-les-murray-says/news-story/8258d4b28507e3684b428a82ef8f56aa |website=Fox Sports Australia |publisher=News Corp |access-date=4 January 2026}}</ref>

