===Australia vs Serbia===
Australia came into the fixture seeking their first win of the tournament. Serbia, having defeated Germany in their previous fixture, only required a draw to progress to the knockout stage.

[[Harry Kewell]], serving a suspension after his sending off against Ghana, was replaced by [[Joshua Kennedy]]. This was the first time Kennedy was included in the starting line-up at this World Cup.<ref name="WestAustAUSSER">{{Cite web |website=The West Australian |last=COOK |first=JONATHAN |date=24 June 2010 |title=Socceroos out despite win over Serbia |url=https://thewest.com.au/sport/soccer/socceroos-out-despite-win-over-serbia-ng-ya-207737}}</ref>

In the opening minutes, Serbia had the better of the chances, with [[Miloš Krasić]] getting into Australia's penalty area and tumbling following his shirt being pulled by [[Carl Valeri]], but appeals for a penalty were waved away. Minutes later, Krasić found himself one-on-one with Australia goalkeeper [[Mark Schwarzer]], but was forced to shoot wide.<ref name="WestAustAUSSER"/>

In the second half, Australia showed more attacking intent, with Mark Bresciano drawing a fine save from Serbia goalkeeper [[Vladimir Stojković]]. After 69 minutes, [[Luke Wilkshire|Luke Wilkshire's]] cross into the penalty box was headed into the Serbia goal by [[Tim Cahill]]. Four minutes later, [[Brett Holman]] doubled the advantage for Australia with a long-range strike.<ref name="WestAustAUSSER"/>

Substitute [[Marko Pantelić]] pulled a goal back for Serbia in the 84th minute after Schwarzer spilled a shot from [[Zoran Tošić]]. Moments later, Pantelić scored what looked like an equalizing goal, but it was cancelled for offside.<ref >{{Cite web |website=Sky Sports |date=23 June 2010 |title=Serbia pay the price |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/australia-vs-serbia/211308}}</ref> At the end of the match, Serbia were denied claims for a penalty when Cahill handballed in his own penalty area after rising for a header.<ref >{{Cite web |last=Hall |first=Matthew | website=The Guardian |date=24 Jun 2010 |title=World Cup 2010: Tim Cahill inspires Australia to victory over Serbia |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/23/australia-serbia-world-cup-match-report}}</ref>

With the game finishing 2–1 to Australia and Ghana losing to Germany 1–0, Australia ended their campaign third in the group due to their inferior goal difference. Germany topped the group to face [[2010 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Germany vs England|England in the round of 16]], and Ghana went on to face [[2010 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#United States vs Ghana|the United States]].

This match was coach [[Pim Verbeek|Pim Verbeek's]] last in charge of Australia.<ref name="WestAustAUSSER"/>
